Division.  In that capacity, Mr. Vargas-Angel assists NOAA in the collection, analysis and publication of information about the revalence and type of coral diseases through out the U.S.-affiliated Pacific Islands.   His work both ashore and at sea materially benefits NOAA by helping to ensure the timely production of monitoring reports that document the state of the coral reef ecosystems in U.S.-affiliated Pacific waters.  Dr. Vargas-Angel does not have access to sensitive information or access to controlled technologies.  He will have no access to restricted NOAA data or technology.',null,null,null,'Permanent Resident Card #094-989-845',null,null,null,null,to_date('04/15/2010 12:32:56','MM/DD/YYYY HH24:MI:SS'),'LSIARIS');
Kewalo Research Facility','United Kingdom','N','Oliver Vetter will serve as a member of the oceanography team on the upcoming Northwestern Hawaiian Islands Reef Assessment and Monitoring Program cruise on the NOAA Ship HI''IALAKAI from 25 August to 28 September 2006.  While onboard, Vetter''s duties and responsibilities will include participating in oceanographic field surveys and the deployment and recovery of various oceanographic instrument platforms via SCUBA and free diving.  Vetter''s current place of work is at the Marine Sciences Building at the University of Hawaii.  Starting 29 September 2006 Oliver Vetter will be working at PIFSC''s Kewalo Research Facility where he will be working with oceanographic equipment and processing oceanographic data.  To perform these tasks he will have access to the division''s oceanographic data repository and other scientific information.',null,'N/A','N',null,null,null,null,null,to_date('09/15/2011 12:48:49','MM/DD/YYYY HH24:MI:SS'),'RAKI');
